A||N||23||msweldon||For you serious suspension gurus......I have some questions (long)||||5||
Z||000000||msweldon||09-26-2000||06:07 PM||||Unbelievable, I've been a Fiero enthusiast since 90' and have never come across this board.  Anyhow, on to my real dilema.<P>I have an 87GT blk/gry purchased in 92' and have done a series of suspension mods to it over the past few years, in the attempt make it the best handling car I could without getting ridiculousy expensive. My attempt is to get the car as neutral as possible through turns, such as a lowered NSX I driven.  I do intend to do some solo SCCA racing so I have become somewhat knowledgable about suspesions. I'm no dummy, but I'm not an expert.  <P>After several thousands of dollars and hours of work I have gotten a really nice handling setup(details to follow) however after taking the car to the limits in controlled situations(empty parking lots w/ cones) I've noticed the car still has a tendency to oversteer slightly on a neutral throttle and as you let off the throttle the car oversteers almost too much.  <P>In a sweeping turn, with the intent to excute a 4-wheel drift I have to apply throttle a bit more than I would like just to keep the car's rear from stepping out.<P>After you read the list of mods I've done please give me your info or advice.<P>Is it my tire size, is it bump steer?<BR>Should I reduce width/aspect of front tires?<BR>Should I have a larger rear bar built?<BR>Should I install a bump steer kit?<P>I dunno?<P>I feel like I've got it so close to what how I thought the fiero "should" handle.<P>Modifications:<BR>Koni Adj. Sport shocks/struts<BR> - front set to med.<BR> - rear set to full stiff<P>Sway Bars w/polyurathane bushings<BR> - 1" front<BR> - 7/8" rear<P>Suspension Techniques Lowering Springs<BR> - (typical lowering ~1.5" front & ~2" rear)<BR>17" OZ MonteCarlo Rims rear<BR>255/40/17 Michilen MXX rear<BR>16" OZ MonteCarlo Rims front<BR>225/50/16 Michilen MXX front (the higher aspect ratio could be the oversteer culprit)<P>new tie-rods, balljoints throughout<P>new Durathane(not quite as hard as polyurathane) bushings throughout.<P>Rear lower A-Arm Herb Adams Stainless Steel bushings<P>non-suspension related:<BR>SpeedWay Brake Kit<BR>Custom true dual exhaust<P>------------------<BR>msweldon<P>87GT - too many suspension mods<BR>& custom true dual exhaust||||reg||5
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||09-26-2000||06:50 PM||||Ok I just looked at your Mod list, and a few things jumped out at me. 1: you didn't list your Tire pressures<P>2: It's lowers 1.5" FRONT & 2" REAR Why is that? I thougt the weight dist. was like 51/49 in favor of the front. I may be wrong<P>Should I reduce width/aspect of front tires?<BR>I would look at the Rear tires first, a 40 series tire has a very stiff side wall. and the way wider rear tire may be "floating" on the pavement, because the tire is so wide and our cars are prety light.  <P>Should I have a larger rear bar <BR>built?<BR>A bigger rear sway bar will give you more oversteer<P>Should I install a bump steer kit<BR>I have no idea, I think all it could do is help.<P>Here Is what I would do, turn down the REAR shocks an click or two. That should give you less oversteer, and match the Front & rear tires for width.<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wannabe||09-26-2000||06:54 PM||||By your description of your knowledge, it's not clear if you would have tried the obvious solutions, so please don't be offended if my suggestions sound simplistic. Have you tried going softer on the shocks in the rear? This will moderately improve rear grip and balance the handling a bit more, since the oversteer seems to be mild. If you have tried, and the oversteer was worse, then you may be getting enough compression in back to bring bump steer into the picture, in that case, go harder in front. It's tough to get used to going hard into high speed sweepers with a car that tends toward oversteer, so you may want to look into RCC's bump steer kit. At $120, it's a relatively inexepensive fix. If you are not using urethane bushings and end links on both front and rear bars, you could try adding them to the front. The slight increase in stiffness will also reduce oversteer. Have you checked tire temps across the treads? 255-40 is a wide, low tire to have on a strut type syspension with no changes to the stock geometry. You may want to adjust camber to make sure you are getting the most out of the rear tires. What about tire pressure? Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Let us know how you make out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-26-2000||07:34 PM||||did you replace the cradle bushings yet? Those crappy old rubber ones are likely about shot if they are still in there. That will let the whole rear of the car shift as engine loads change.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||09-26-2000||08:07 PM||||as i am just now learning about suspension's, i want waist much time, but i do have a couple of questions.  you said youi replaced the bushings, but not knowing which one's...<P>1)did you change the cradle bushings? (yes ogre, you brought it up first, but it is a excellent point)  if so, what did you use?  1fst2m6 (forum member) has aluminum bushings available, and these will remove all of the cradle movement.<BR>2)did you replace the engine/trany mount bushings/struts?  polyurethane is available for these as well (chris west, west coast fieros has them).  this will eliminate a lot of the movement produced by the engine, on the cradle.<BR>3) what was the goal in lowering the rear more than the front?  i would think that that would change the ballance of the whole car.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||gixxer||09-26-2000||08:52 PM||||I would agree with the board about the too stiff rear shocks. You may want to try a little bit more toe-in in the back. The rear toe tends to decrease when you back off the throttle because of engine braking. When the power is on, the x-tra toe-in would help steer the higher loaded outside rear tire a bit more to the inside of the curve, reducing the apparent oversteer. I used this toe-in stuff on my old fat tired Corvair. Never had the occasion to try it on my Fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||msweldon||09-26-2000||10:25 PM||||Thanks for all of the help fellas,<P>I'll start with reducing the front aspect to a 225/45 or a 205/50, fronts need  replacing anyhow.  After that I'll attempt the bump steer kit and cradle/engine bushings.<P>tire psi is 32 front and rear. <P>I've backed off the rear shocks from soft to hard and the oversteer only gets worse the softer the koni's are.  I have strong reason to believe that it may be due to the cradle and engine mounts.  I can feel the engine/cradle move rather severely under high torque scenarios, which may be altering the suspension geometry under load conditions like the lower A-arm bushings did at one time.<P>What's RCC's web site?<P>------------------<BR>msweldon<P>87GT - too many suspension mods<BR>& custom true dual exhaust||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-26-2000||10:41 PM||||The cradle moving will definatly change the rear angles. Likely all of them depending on exactly how it moves.<P>Do the cradle first then worry about engine mounts and/or the bump steer kit. You'll want to see what diferance that makes before adding the bump kit. I was really suprised how much differance polly cradle bushings made. It's like a whole diferant car and my old bushings were in reasonably decent shape. (Also keep in mind. Solid or polly cradle mounts WILL effect any rear sway bar that is installed. The sway bar will have a much firmer foundation then with the OE cradle bushings. Just how much effect I couldn't say.)<P>For race use I'd put solid cradle mounts in. They will eliminate all engine induced cradle movent. With solid bushings the engine can't use the dog bone to shift the cradle no matter what type engine mounts are in it or how hard you lean on it. <P>I don't recomend polly motor mounts for a car that is driven on the street. You'll get a bunch more vibration transmitted thru the car. Once the cradle is locked in the OE style rubber mounts will work the way they where ment to.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||msweldon||09-27-2000||10:01 AM||||Would the poly engine mounts really make the ride that much more rough?  The car already rides like a coal truck so I can't imagine the ride being any worse.<P>Questions for the masses:<P>Does suspension techniques make a stiffer rear strut spring?<P>What's RCC's web site?<P>Many thanks,<BR>Marc||||reg||1
Z||000009||elfiero||09-27-2000||10:47 AM||||something the others have not touched on,a<BR>fiero has approximately 57% rear weight.This<BR>will cause neutral throttle oversteer.You<BR>have started to combat the problem correctly<BR>with your tire selection,but now you need to<BR>move stuff around.1.move the battery to the<BR>front under the spare battery(see V8 archie<BR>for the tray).2.Get rid of everything from<BR>the "trunk"-including carpet.Everything<BR>behind the centerline of the rear axle acts<BR>like a pendulum in a turn.3.Add lead to the<BR>front end close to the ground-I know it's counterproductive to make the car heavyer,but you said you wanted a cheap way<BR>to make it "feel" more stable.After these<BR>tricks it starts to get expensive,so try it<BR>and get back to me,and i'll give you some more ideas.||||reg||6
Z||000010||Will||09-27-2000||12:02 PM||||Try each of these suggestions. If one doesn't work, try the next one.<P>1) Aluminum cradle bushings. Chris west sells these, 1fst2m6 has them, and they are not hard to make, either.<P>2) rod-end sway bar links on front end, and/or 1 1/8 front swaybar.<P>3)Polyurethane engine mounts. If you already have hard pivots on rear control arms, the additional roughness from urethane engine mounts probably won't be noticed.<P>4) Bump steer eliminator kit. <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ryanemotorsports.com</A>  has one if you move quickly. I also hear that RCC has one, but I don't know their website, but I heard found this address (current?):<BR>RCC Specialty Products<BR>5609-F Parker Henderson Road<BR>Fort Worth, TX 76119<BR>817.536.3820 <P>I would advise against going to smaller tires. The smaller your tires, the less ultimate grip you have. I prefer to use the biggest tires that will fit, balance them approximately front and rear, and use chassis techniques to fine-tune the handling.<BR>Is 225 the widest tire you can fit in front? I estimate that a 245/45-17 can be used if you get the back-spacing right and don't mind a little rubbing at large steering angles. The smaller aspect ratio would help balance them with rear tires<P>I am running 235/60-15's front and 245/60-15's rear on stock wheels on my '86 SE. They are cheap tires at the moment (Dunlop G/T qualifier), so experiences with better tires may differ. I am using '88 front suspension with stock 1 1/8 front sway-bar, and original 7/8 front way bar on rear, with rubber end links.<BR>     The car is neutral on throttle, exhibits very close to neutral behavior at neutral throttle, but still has snap oversteer at abrupt lift throttle or brake application.<BR>     I've also got worn out shocks all around and right front wheel bearing in need of replacement, stock bushings on rear control arms, stock cradle bushings (not for long,though), etc.||||reg||1
Z||000011||3400Fiero||09-27-2000||12:42 PM||||This is a great thread! very informative.<P>In my 88 formula I have poly bushings and end links all four corners, 1-1/4" front sway bar, 1" rear sway bar, springs cut (not the best, but cheap) about 1-1/2", oem replacement shocks and struts.  I have mild understeer under racing conditions ie autocross (running 245/45/16 Toyo RA-1s all around, 18psi front 30psi rear).  I have considered putting the stock front bar back on to get the steering more neutral, but have experienced some oversteer around town (street tires), so I think I will leave it alone unless you guys can think of any improvements? <P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||09-27-2000||02:03 PM||||I forgot something.<BR>In my last post, make moving the battery up front under spare tire #2 and bump everything after down a notch.||||reg||11
Z||000013||1FST2M6||09-27-2000||04:22 PM||||AAAGH!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> i've seen you around a few times... I had 255s on my old wheels and the 245s i replaced them with were nicer riding but on the track my best numbers were with the stock 14s and Yokohama A032s. another Fiero enthusist (sp) George Palm of "the Van shop" and i have raced a few times and I SMOKED his car and hes got a 3.2... at Road ATL with a trained driver it'll hold 1.14g and with me driving i'd spill it at about .98 or 1g.. i'm "not smooth enough" have you taken your car to little talladega or roebling road? what are your times? my rear shocks wer about 1.5 turns from full hard. and the front was right in the middle... running -1.25 camber in all 4 corners. with poly cradle bushings. Thanks for the Plug Bhoop!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   might want to check on a sticky tire in stead of a nice tire.. the Yokohama A032s, A008s, Nexus, and AVS intermediates are all SUPER sticky.. but don't expect them to last long. move you battery to the front! <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  and get his relocater kit. where in marietta are you?   I my office is on the Square and live off of Pine Mtn Rd. in kennesaw. had a Gold 86 SE with Engineered Performance Sticker on the windoes.. and NOS on the sail panels... (currently owned by forum member GT Bastard)   <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 09-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Xtreme Fiero's||09-28-2000||12:01 AM||||What is the Weight dist. on the Notchback and the Fastback they have to be diffrent. I had it backwards in my first post. I know that the rear is heaver than the front but how much. <P>msweldon: i'm sill wondering why did you lower the  back more than the front? I stil don't get that? <P>I had planed on a 2.5"F/1.5"R drop on my car to help balance the car  out and get rid of over-steer. Am I wrong? I also plan on adding a ste of 16" wheels W/ 225/40/16's Yokohama AVS's ||||reg||1
Z||000015||1FST2M6||09-28-2000||06:04 PM||||you might want to re think that Xtreme... a 225-50 would be better.. there is a saying.. "..point of deminishing return.." and a 40 series tire will have a tenency to break loose before the side wall flexes. you'll have almost NO slip angle. a 225-50 will keep your speedo closer to what your really running, give you a better ride AND hold better in a corner... and the GT is about 150lbs heavyier. <P>215/.60/25.4*2+15=25.16<BR>225/.40/25.4*2+16=23.08 <BR>225/.50/25.4*2+16=24.85 (tire height)<P>you'll LOVE thos AVSs though!!! have a set on a Alfa 164S and OH HOW SWEET! a 3500lb car that is in the same Solo II class as a mustang and turns the same numbers with 3liter V6 and 4 doors! they are great tires.. they can be noisey though.. but WORTH IT!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 09-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wannabe||09-28-2000||06:20 PM||||3400 Fiero, why so low on the tire pressures with the RA1s? These are street radials with a sticky rubber compound, so increasing pressure improves grip. You can run these as high as 50psi cool. With your starting pressures so low, you will be amazed at the dramatic changes you can make in the handling by raising the pressures.||||reg||1
Z||000017||3400Fiero||09-29-2000||05:03 AM||||I used to run ~30psi all around, but a friend of mine who races a 1979 Porsche 911 (autocross) runs 12psi F and 22 psi R on Yokohama 032R's.  He suggested I try lowering the fronts - I did and I couldn't believe the difference it made to the understeer problem I was having before.  Now it is balanced quite well as long as the race is long enough for the tires to warm.  Seems to work.  They are 245/45/16 - too big if you ask me, but they were cheap (used).  I think 225/50/16 is a very good size.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||3400Fiero||09-29-2000||05:06 AM||||I forgot to mention that I only run this setup at autocross.  For street I switch back to Toyo FZ4's 225/50/16 and run 32 psi all around.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||msweldon||09-29-2000||08:59 AM||||XTreme:<P>What ever the standard drop produced by the Suspension Techniques springs sold with the IRM suspension kit, is what I got.  I just vaguely remembering reading that the setup lowered the rear a tad bit more than the front.<P>Does anyone know where I can replace my front 16" OZ MonteCarlo with a 17" of the same type.  I don't think OZ makes the Monte Carlo's anymore.  I could be wrong.  I hope so...<P>thanks for all of the feedback fellas<BR>Marc||||reg||1
Z||000020||1FST2M6||09-29-2000||01:48 PM||||OZ stil makes them... <A HREF="http://www.modernauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.modernauto.com</A>  has them... the suspension tech kit is 1.5 in all 4 corners... ||||reg||1
Z||000021||msweldon||09-29-2000||02:29 PM||||1FST2M6,<P>I've been talking to Travis Hall over the last few days and hope to see you at the Mustang Dyno Day next weekend.<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000022||Xtreme Fiero's||09-29-2000||09:01 PM||||<BR>1FST2M6: I have 215/60/14's stock so is 225/50/16 still the right size to use? I figured 225/30/16 would be the closest to the O.E. size. I think your math is based on me having a 15" wheel stock. pleas let me know if i'm wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||09-29-2000||11:51 PM||||Sounds like you're running out of travel,or just too stiff on the springs.more sidewall in front will just make it easier to control while in oversteer mode,in fact,you might go smaller in front,I really like the 205/55-16 in front,and 225/50-16 in the rear.A rear swaybar that has binding outer bushings(ie,not heim jointed)can cause quick oversteer also.Good luck.||||reg||1
